<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Donaueschingen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Donaueschingen is a town in the Black Forest-Baar district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, at the meeting of the Brigach and Breg, which form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. From the 18th century it was the residence of the princes of Fürstenberg, whose palace park contains a spring regarded as the source of the Danube. The town has held a festival of contemporary music since 1921.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/donaueschingen.jpg" alt="Donaueschingen" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JoachimKohler-HB · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Donaueschingen_Lammplatz_und_Stadtkirche_(2025).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Donaueschingen lies in the Baar basin in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-forest">Black Forest</a>, in the Schwarzwald-Baar district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>. It stands about 13 kilometres south of Villingen-Schwenningen, 24 kilometres west of Tuttlingen and about 30 kilometres north of the Swiss town of Schaffhausen. The town takes its name from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, German Donau, which begins at the confluence here of the Brigach and the Breg, its two source tributaries. An enclosed karst spring in the park of Donaueschingen Palace, the source of the Donaubach, is also known as the source of the Danube. The heights of the municipal area fall from 1,020 metres in the Black Forest to about 660 metres on the Baar plateau and rise again above 900 metres at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-jura">Swabian Alb</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The place is first recorded as Esginga in 889, and the modern form of its name is first attested in 1292. In 1283 Rudolph von <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> granted the principality of Baar and Donaueschingen to Heinrich von Fürstenberg, a grant that carried the right to brew beer, which is the origin of the Fürstenberg Brewery. In 1488 possession passed to the Count of Fürstenberg-Baar, and from the 18th century the town was the residence of the princes of Fürstenberg, who lived in Schloss Donaueschingen.</p><p>In 1806 it came under the rule of the Grand Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> and received township in 1810. A large part of the town was destroyed by fire in 1908. It has a tradition as a military garrison: the French military had barracks there from 1945 to 2014, and until the early 1990s the United States Air Force ran a contingency hospital, which was most used in 1989, when it housed refugees leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> for the West. The Fürstenberg princes were nominally mediatised and deposed as absolute rulers, but they kept much property, including the palace and its gardens. They owned an important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nibelungenlied">Nibelungenlied</a>, Manuscript C, until they sold it in 2001.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The town had 21,750 people in 2015 and is a regional rail hub where four lines join, including the Schwarzwaldbahn and the Höllentalbahn to Freiburg. The long-distance Danube Cycle Trail starts there. The Donaueschingen Festival for contemporary music, founded in 1921 and one of the world's oldest festivals for new music, takes place every October, and guest composers have included Paul Hindemith, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnold-schoenberg">Arnold Schoenberg</a>, Pierre Boulez, John Cage and György Ligeti. The Fürstenberg Library holds early copies of five of Mozart's operas, an important source for reviving historically informed performance of 18th-century opera.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Donaueschingen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Count Henry took the name Fürstenberg, after a castle near <b>Donaueschingen</b>, around 1250, founding a comital house that ruled the upper Danube and Kinzig valleys.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-furstenberg">Principality of Fürstenberg</a></li></ul></section>
